// Native backend: a `StrBuilder` field inside a struct.
//
// KNOWN LEAK — 112 bytes (the builder shell plus its buffer). Deliberately
// outside the zero-leak fixture set; it is here so the behaviour is executed
// and recorded rather than silently unexercised.
//
// Why it leaks, and why it is not simply "fixed":
//
//   * `Builder` is a shared handle (`is_shared_handle`), where clone is
//     identity. A by-value struct copy therefore aliases one builder, so a
//     per-copy destructor free is a double free — that is what crashed
//     json_mako_test and why 07cc81f removed `IrType::Builder` from
//     `emit_struct_drop`.
//   * With no destructor free, a builder created directly into a struct field
//     has no owner of record, so nothing releases it. That is this leak.
//   * `mako_str_builder_string()` does not release the builder either: it
//     copies the bytes into a fresh string and leaves the shell and buffer
//     intact. (`mako_str_builder_finish()` is the one that steals the buffer
//     and frees the shell.) So the fix is not "let builder_string consume it".
//
// Closing this needs an owner-of-record for shared handles held in aggregates
// — refcounting, or a move into the struct that transfers the sole owner —
// not another destructor tweak. Reinstating the destructor free trades this
// leak for a double-free crash, which is the worse failure.

struct Acc {
    buf: StrBuilder
    tag: int
}

fn main() {
    let a = Acc { buf: str_builder(), tag: 4 }
    builder_write(a.buf, "ma")
    builder_write(a.buf, "ko")
    print(builder_string(a.buf))
    print_int(a.tag)
}
